ISR (In-Sync Replicas)

ISR stands for In-Sync Replicas.

When Kafka replicates data, each partition has:

Leader

Followers (replicas)

Example:

Partition-0

Leader → Broker 1
Replica → Broker 2
Replica → Broker 3

Followers replicate data from the leader.

ISR Definition

ISR = replicas that are fully synchronized with the leader.

Example:

Leader (Broker1)
ISR = Broker1, Broker2
Out-of-sync = Broker3

Broker3 might be slow or disconnected.

Why ISR is Important

Kafka only considers ISR replicas for leader election.

If leader fails:

New Leader ← selected from ISR

This prevents data loss.

Important Config
min.insync.replicas

Example:

replication.factor = 3
min.insync.replicas = 2

Meaning:

Producer writes succeed only if 2 replicas acknowledge.

2️⃣ Exactly-Once Delivery

Kafka normally guarantees at-least-once delivery.

That means duplicates may occur.

Example:

Consumer processes message
Consumer crashes before committing offset
Message processed again

Duplicate occurs.

Exactly-Once Solution

Kafka supports exactly-once semantics (EOS) using:

1️⃣ Idempotent producer
2️⃣ Transactions

Idempotent Producer

Configuration:

enable.idempotence=true

Kafka ensures:

Duplicate messages are not written
Kafka Transactions

Example:

Read message
Process message
Write result to another topic
Commit offset

All operations succeed or fail together.

3️⃣ Kafka Fault Tolerance

Kafka is designed for high fault tolerance.

Key mechanisms:

Replication

Example:

Topic Partition
   |
   |-- Leader
   |-- Replica
   |-- Replica

Replication factor usually:

3
Leader Election

If leader fails:

Replica becomes leader

Service continues.

Data Persistence

Kafka stores logs on disk.

Even if consumers crash:

Messages remain in Kafka
Producer Acknowledgements

Producer config:

acks=all

Meaning:

Write succeeds only when all ISR replicas confirm
